.TH NP_PIPELINE 3 "June 2026" "netpipe v0.1.0" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
np_pipeline_new, np_pipeline_free, np_pipeline_run, np_pipeline_stop \- netpipe pipeline lifecycle management
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <netpipe.h>
.PP
.BI "np_pipeline_t *np_pipeline_new(void);"
.br
.BI "void np_pipeline_free(np_pipeline_t *" pl ");"
.br
.BI "np_err_t np_pipeline_run(np_pipeline_t *" pl ");"
.br
.BI "void np_pipeline_stop(np_pipeline_t *" pl ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions manage the lifecycle and execution of a netpipe pipeline context.
.PP
The
.BR np_pipeline_new ()
function allocates and initializes a new, empty pipeline context.
It returns a pointer to the allocated
.B np_pipeline_t
struct, or
.B NULL
if an error occurs (such as memory exhaustion).
.PP
The
.BR np_pipeline_free ()
function stops the pipeline if it is running, releases all resources associated with the pipeline context, and frees the context itself.
Any sources, filters, processors, and sinks added to the pipeline are automatically freed when the pipeline is freed; the caller must not free them individually.
If
.I pl
is
.BR NULL ,
no action is taken.
.PP
The
.BR np_pipeline_run ()
function starts processing network packets.
It runs the main event loop, reading packets from all configured sources, passing them through filters and processors, and sending them to sinks.
By default, this call is blocking and does not return until the pipeline is stopped using
.BR np_pipeline_stop ()
or an unrecoverable error occurs.
.PP
The
.BR np_pipeline_stop ()
function requests the running pipeline to shut down.
It signals the pipeline's event loop to terminate, which will cause
.BR np_pipeline_run ()
to complete its current packet processing and return.
This function is thread-safe and can be safely called from a signal handler (e.g., to handle SIGINT/Ctrl-C) or from another thread.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.BR np_pipeline_new ()
returns a pointer to the newly allocated pipeline context, or
.B NULL
on failure.
.PP
.BR np_pipeline_run ()
returns
.B NP_OK
on clean shutdown, or an error code of type
.B np_err_t
on failure (see
.BR libnetpipe (3)
for details).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libnetpipe (3),
.BR np_source (3),
.BR np_filter (3),
.BR np_processor (3),
.BR np_sink (3)
